# Navigation and Back-Control Rules

## Status

**Decided by the owner. This is settled — do not relitigate.** Every screen must offer a way back,
and it must be the **same on-brand control everywhere** — not a bespoke pill, text link, or arrow an
agent invents for one screen. This has been shipped wrong more than once (a stray "Back to admin"
pill on the AI Review Dashboard, a stacked back-bar, a screen with no back at all), so it is now a
hard rule. Read this before adding, moving, or "fixing" any back button or screen header.

**Policy update (owner-directed, 2026-07-17): back is history-first.** The in-app back control now
returns to the **previous in-app page** when there is one, and only falls back to the tailored
one-skill-up destination when there is no in-app history (e.g. the installed web app opened straight
onto a deep link). The earlier "always one-skill-up, never history" rule is superseded. The shared
pieces implement this — nothing changes for callers, and the ban on hand-rolled back controls stands.

**Mobile-first update (owner decision, 2026-07-20 — see rule 105):** the web app now renders the
phone-width layout at every viewport, so the phone-width behavior described below is what every web
visitor sees. Mentions of "desktop" below describe the now-hidden desktop branches (`useIsMobile` is
pinned to mobile); they are kept for context. Do not add new desktop-only navigation or rails.

## The one shared way (mandatory)

There are exactly three shared pieces. Use them; never hand-roll a back control.

1. **`MobileScreenHeader`** (`components/shared/mobile-screen-header.tsx`) — the standard top bar:
   an accent-tinted rounded-square **back chevron** on the far left, the plugin's **brand icon**, the
   **screen title**, and the shared **report-bug / settings / account** cluster (`MobileTopActions`)
   on the right. It **self-gates on the breakpoint**: at phone width it renders the full bar; on
   desktop it renders only a slim back bar (or nothing when `desktopBack={false}`). Drop it in
   unconditionally and let it pick the right bar. Its optional `actions` prop renders extra header
   controls (e.g. an admin surface's "Member view" link) on both breakpoints.
2. **`PluginRailFooter`** (`components/shared/plugin-rail-footer.tsx`) — the bottom of a plugin's
   **desktop left icon rail**: back, account & settings, and the account menu. A screen that
   has a desktop icon rail already gets its desktop back control from this footer.
3. **`BackChevronButton`** (`lib/nav/back-history.tsx`) — the standalone accent-tinted chevron for a
   member shell that builds its own phone-width header instead of using `MobileScreenHeader`. Use it
   in place of any hand-rolled `<Link href="/apps">` chevron.

All three resolve behavior through **`useSmartBack()`** (`lib/nav/back-history.tsx`): previous
in-app page when the per-tab history stack has one (real `router.back()`, so scroll/state restore),
else the one-skill-up fallback from **`resolveBackTarget(pathname)`** (`lib/nav/back-target.ts` —
an `/admin/*` page → `/admin`; `/admin` → home; `/account/*` → `/account`; everything else → all
apps). The history stack is maintained by **`NavHistoryTracker`**, mounted once in the root layout —
do not mount it again. **Never compute a back destination by hand** and never call `router.back()`
directly outside `useSmartBack` (raw history back can exit the app when there is no in-app history).

## Admin ↔ member navigation (mandatory pair)

- **Every admin surface links to its plugin's member shell** via the shared
  **`PluginUserShellButton`** (`components/shared/plugin-user-shell-button.tsx`) — a small
  accent-tinted "Member view" pill in the admin header (pass it through `MobileScreenHeader`'s
  `actions` prop on rail-less admin shells). Admin surfaces whose member experience is the Commons
  (comic review, feed announcements) link to `/`.
- **Every member plugin shell shows the shared `PluginAdminButton`** (admin-only, renders nothing
  for non-admins) in its header when the plugin has an admin surface, linking to `/admin/<area>`.

## How to wire it (by screen shape)

- **Rail-less screen (most admin shells, simple pages):** render `<MobileScreenHeader title=… accent=…
  icon=… actions={<PluginUserShellButton …/>} />` as the first element. It carries the back control on
  both breakpoints. Done. Follow a shipped sibling such as `socket-relay-admin-shell.tsx` or
  `bug-reports-admin-shell.tsx`.
- **Screen with a desktop left icon rail (e.g. the AI Review Dashboard, community shell):** the rail's
  `PluginRailFooter` is the desktop back, and the rail is hidden at phone width — so render
  `<MobileScreenHeader … desktopBack={false} />` so the mobile header appears on phones and nothing
  duplicates on desktop, or use `BackChevronButton` inside the shell's own phone-width header. When
  the screen is a full-height flex/grid, wrap it so the sticky header sits above the content and the
  panes fill the remaining space (`height: 100%` of a `flex: 1` parent, not a raw `100vh`/`100dvh`
  that would overflow by the header's height). Follow `contributions-shell.tsx`.
- **Android (React Native):** the app already shows a persistent top navigator on every screen, so a
  member always has a way back. Do not add a second bespoke back control there; keep parity by relying
  on that navigator.

## Forbidden (never ship these)

- A hand-rolled back affordance: a custom "Back to …" pill/button, a bare arrow, a text link, or a raw
  `<Link href="/apps">` styled as a back control, when `MobileScreenHeader` / `PluginRailFooter` /
  `BackChevronButton` already cover the screen.
- A screen with **no** way back at phone width (the icon rail is hidden on mobile — that is exactly
  when the shared mobile header is required).
- A second, stacked, or duplicated back control (e.g. a mobile header **and** a bespoke pill; a desktop
  rail back **and** a desktop `MobileScreenHeader` back — use `desktopBack={false}` to opt out).
- Inventing the back destination or label inline, calling `router.back()` directly, or mounting a
  second `NavHistoryTracker`.

## Why this exists

Members and admins are non-technical and use the app mostly on a phone (often installed to the home
screen, where there is no browser back button). A screen with no back, or with a different-looking
back on every screen, strands them. One shared control in one place means the way back is always in
the same spot, looks the same everywhere, and — since 2026-07-17 — behaves like the back button
members already know: it returns to where they just were, not to a fixed hub.
